  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-121831",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,r & =2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\,r & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={30}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={150}^{∘}.$  \n\nNotice that we must have $r=1$ for any point that lies on both curves. \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{30}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )=(1,{150}^{∘}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
